          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "Having required multiple courses of steroids can indicate a potential issue with asthma control, but this does not automatically mean you have poor control. It is essential to assess both your symptoms and treatment strategy. According to asthma management guidelines, if a patient has poor control, which might include frequent symptoms, nighttime awakenings, or the need for rescue inhaler use, stepping up therapy should be considered.\n\nGuidance typically suggests several approaches for stepping up treatment, including checking inhaler technique, increasing the dose of inhaled corticosteroids, or adding an additional medication. It is important to have regular reviews with your healthcare provider to ensure the right steps are taken based on your specific situation.\n\nI could not cover the specific details of your inhalation technique or your individual treatment history. It would be best to discuss these concerns directly with your clinician for personalized advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
